WEBVTT

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:11.311
Hi there.

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:15.599
Welcome to this video where we are going
to learn what social engineering is.

00:00:16.351 --> 00:00:21.039
The goal of this video is to explain
social engineering in a clear and simple way.

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:26.591
We will look at what it is,
how it works, and why attackers use it.

00:00:27.231 --> 00:00:32.639
Understanding this 
helps you stay safer online.

00:00:35.423 --> 00:00:36.943
Social engineering is a way

00:00:36.943 --> 00:00:39.935
attackers trick people 
instead of hacking computers.

00:00:40.143 --> 00:00:44.703
When people hear about cyber attacks,
they often think about computers and code.

00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:48.095
But many attacks
do not start with technology.

00:00:48.095 --> 00:00:51.103
They start with people and their behavior.

00:00:51.823 --> 00:00:54.063
People trust others, want to help,

00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:57.071
and sometimes feel 
pressure to act quickly.

00:00:57.183 --> 00:01:00.191
Attackers know this 
and use it to their advantage.

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:03.343
This is why people are often the target.

00:01:07.023 --> 00:01:09.903
Social engineering
is when someone tricks or manipulates

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:13.583
people into giving away
information, access, or money.

00:01:14.095 --> 00:01:17.743
It focuses on human behavior,
not technical weaknesses.

00:01:18.463 --> 00:01:22.463
Instead of breaking into a system,
attackers convince someone to help them.

00:01:22.623 --> 00:01:25.615
This can be faster
and easier than hacking.

00:01:25.743 --> 00:01:28.623
The victim may not even realize
an attack is happening.

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:37.151
Attackers may pretend 
to be friendly, helpful, or important.

00:01:37.423 --> 00:01:41.503
They may create urgency or fear
to stop people from thinking carefully.

00:01:42.031 --> 00:01:45.023
Sometimes they offer rewards or prizes.

00:01:45.791 --> 00:01:48.031
Attackers usually have clear goals.

00:01:48.031 --> 00:01:51.023
They want information, access, or money.

00:01:51.423 --> 00:01:54.431
Social engineering
helps them reach these goals.

00:01:55.151 --> 00:01:59.823
Information can include passwords, names,
email addresses, or school accounts.

00:02:00.463 --> 00:02:03.935
With this information, attackers
can cause more problems later.

00:02:04.543 --> 00:02:07.535
That is why information must be protected.

00:02:07.615 --> 00:02:10.815
Access means being able 
to log in or use an account.

00:02:11.583 --> 00:02:15.023
Once attackers have access,
they can pretend to be someone else.

00:02:15.215 --> 00:02:18.223
This can lead to serious problems.

00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:20.287
Sometimes attackers want money.

00:02:20.287 --> 00:02:23.887
This could be real money,
gift cards or in-game currency.

00:02:24.495 --> 00:02:30.127
Social engineering is often used 
to trick people into sending it.

00:02:32.751 --> 00:02:35.631
Social engineering
can happen online or offline.

00:02:35.631 --> 00:02:39.839
It can happen in messages, emails,
games, phone calls, or face to face.

00:02:40.446 --> 00:02:43.454
The same tricks 
can be used in many places.

00:02:43.566 --> 00:02:46.366
Social engineering works
because people are human.

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:50.254
People trust, feel emotions
and sometimes act quickly.

00:02:50.974 --> 00:02:52.926
Attackers take advantage of this.

00:02:52.926 --> 00:02:56.334
Understanding social engineering 
helps you stop and think.

00:02:57.086 --> 00:03:00.574
If something feels strange 
or urgent, slow down.

00:03:00.574 --> 00:03:02.734
Thinking first can help you stay safe.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:05.342
Great work!

00:03:05.342 --> 00:03:09.742
In this video you learned what social
engineering is and why attackers use it.

00:03:10.606 --> 00:03:12.174
Thank you for watching.

00:03:12.174 --> 00:03:15.182
Do not forget to watch other videos
in our series.